rker v3.4.0 (deployed this morning) was unhealthy in all production regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west) with an 18% error rate. Rolled back to v3.3.0, the most recent release that was healthy in production; the rollback is complete in all regions. payments-api (v5.1.0) also shows as unhealthy with an 18% error rate. A rollback of payments-api was proposed and denied by the approver, who identified payments-worker as the source. Follow up: confirm payments-api recovers now that the worker is rolled back, and find the root cause of the v3.4.0 failure.", "idempotency_key": "payments-worker-v3.4.0-rollback-sev2"}, "name": "create_incident", "type": "tool_use", "toolset_name": null}]}, {"stop_reason": "end_turn", "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I rolled payments-worker back to v3.3.0 and opened a SEV2 incident (INC-0001), which paged on-call.\n\n1. **Finding the service:** There's no service called \"payments.\" payments-api was the first match I found: v5.1.0 was unhealthy, with an 18% error rate. I tried to roll it back to v5.0.0, but the approver denied it and said the problem is in payments-worker.\n2. **Rollback:** This morning's deploy, payments-worker v3.4.0, is marked unhealthy, with an 18% error rate in all three regions (us-east, us-west, eu-west). v3.3.0 is its most recent release that was healthy in production. The approver signed off on the rollback, and it's now live in all three regions.\n3. **Incident:** INC-0001 is open for payments-worker. It says payments-api also looked unhealthy and that its rollback was denied. It lists two follow-ups: confirm payments-api recovers now that the worker is back on v3.3.0, and find out why v3.4.0 failed.\n\nNothing changed on payments-api; it's still on v5.1.0. I haven't re-checked error rates for either service since the rollback, so that's the next thing to look at.", "type": "text"}]}]}